
        <h4 class="text-center">A note (and a plea) from the gradeomatic's creator</h4>
        <p class="answer">Thank you for your interest in the gradeomatic.</p>
        <p class="answer">I created this tool to help me save time without sacrificing the quality of student feedback. And, I did. I've cut my grading time by 60-70%.</p>
        <p class="answer">Along the way, I discovered that it helped me improve my teaching. The gradeomatic collects large amounts of granular data on every exam. This helps me determine whether a new assignment or way of presenting a concept actually benefits my students.</p>
        <p class="answer">I was also surprised to discover that I became more willing to experiment. I had always worried that if I tried something new, I would hurt my students. I worried that I wouldn't be able to tell if a class's poor performance was my fault. With the gradeomatic, I can see when to declare instructor error, bump up scores accordingly, and go back to the drawing board next semester. Best of all, I can do this without giving the same exam every year. That reduces the risk of cheating and lets me keep the course content fresh.
        </p>
        <p class="answer">I sincerely hope the gradeomatic will also help you grade faster and teach better. However, teaching resists standardization in myriad ways. Ours is an incredibly personal and personalized profession. I've tried to make the gradeomatic extremely flexible while still being usable. But it can be better. Thus the plea in the title is for your feedback. I will be grateful for every suggestion and every criticism, large or small, kindhearted or otherwise.</p>
        <p class="answer">Please let me know how the gradeomatic can be improved (and what works well for you) at {{ env('CONTACT_EMAIL') }}.</p>
        <p class="answer">Thank you,<br/>
            Adam Swenson, Ph.D.<br/>
            Associate Professor of Philosophy
        </p>

        <p class="answer">P.S., I confess I have met my match when it comes to writing the instructions. "How hard could it be?", I had asked myself "After all, I explain difficult things in clear and accessible ways for a living." But once I sat down and started writing, I quickly discovered how horribly, horribly wrong I had been.</p>
        <p class="answer">These instructions will probably be most comprehensible if you start by just playing around with the gradeomatic. If you do, please make sure to not use real student email addresses to avoid inadvertently sending your class messages!</p>
        <p class="answer">I will continue to work to make the instructions clear and helpful. In the meantime, I will greatly appreciate your comments and patience, and urge those of you with technical writers in your lives to buy them a drink. They deserve it. </p>
